@page "/components/gradient-border"

<PageTitle>GradientBorder — Unpoly.Blazor.Shadcn</PageTitle>

<h1 class="doc-h1">GradientBorder</h1>
<p class="doc-lede">
    A 1px gradient edge around a panel. Pure CSS — the gradient plate sits one pixel behind a
    surface-coloured inset, so the child's own layout never shifts.
</p>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Basic</h2>
<Example Source="GradientBorderExample.razor"><GradientBorderExample /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Around a card</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    The usual pairing. The child brings its own surface, so the default
    <code>bg-background</code> underneath is simply covered — pass <code>Surface</code> when it is
    not.
</p>
<Example Source="GradientBorderCard.razor"><GradientBorderCard /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Restyle it from the outside</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    There are no colour parameters. <code>Class</code> is merged with tailwind-merge, so a
    caller's colour-stop or gradient-direction class replaces the recipe's rather than sitting
    beside it.
</p>

<h2 class="doc-h2">When not to use it</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    The gradient is decoration and nothing more. It cannot be announced, it is invisible at 1px
    on a low-contrast display, and it flattens to one colour wherever colour mixing is not
    dependable. Never let a border be the only signal that a state exists: a selected panel, a
    validation failure or a required field must say so in text and in the control's own
    <code>Variant</code>.
</p>
